[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a form declare its validation rules? → Rule arrays (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the scope of the abstraction itself. Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m hand-wires [38;5;153mgetElementById[39m per field and reports errors only to
  [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m — nothing is shown on the page. Duplicating [3mthat[0m across signup is as much of a problem as duplicating
  the rules.

  - [1mPure validation only[0m — the module exports [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m plus the rules; each form keeps its own code to
    read inputs and show messages. Smallest surface, trivially testable. But every new form rewrites the same
    read-fields/show-errors loop, so the copy-paste just moves.
  - [1mValidation + a thin form binder[0m — additionally [38;5;153mbindForm(formElement, schema, onValid)[39m: it reads the form's named
    inputs, runs [38;5;153mvalidate[39m, renders messages next to the offending fields, and calls your callback only when clean. A new
    form becomes an HTML form plus a schema. Costs: it takes a position on error markup and on when validation runs.
  - [1mValidation + a render helper[0m — [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m plus [38;5;153mrenderErrors(formElement, errors)[39m, but each form still writes its own
    submit handler.

  [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m stays independently usable in all three, so the binder is additive — adopting it doesn't trap you, and a
  form can opt out and call [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m directly.

  I recommend [1mvalidation + a thin form binder[0m, with error display kept overridable. Without it, "reusable across
  multiple forms" only solves a third of what's actually repeated — and it's also the piece that finally puts validation
  errors in front of the user instead of in the console.
